          "question": "I have had three headaches this month, all on one side, with nausea and the light bothering me.",
          "answer": "I can't tell you what is causing these headaches. I can share what the reference material says about this pattern.\n\n**What the material describes.** The Migraine reference (General clinical reference) says a migraine headache is often one-sided, pulsating, and made worse by routine activity. It is often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, or sensitivity to light and sound. Your description matches that pattern in several ways. The same reference notes that headaches are not always one-sided, so the pattern alone does not settle it.\n\n**Medication use.** The migraine guideline (National headache society guidance) warns that using pain relief very often can keep headaches going. It lists limits for different kinds of medicine. If you have been taking pain relief for these headaches, mention how often to a clinician.\n\n**Emergency check.** The urgent-symptom check found no emergency pattern in what you described. It also says this is not a clearance. Get emergency help right away if you notice any of these:\n- A sudden, severe headache that reaches full strength within a minute, or the worst headache of your life.\n- Headache with fever and a stiff neck.\n- Face drooping, arm or leg weakness, slurred speech, sudden confusion, or sudden vision loss.\n- Headache after a head injury.\n\n**Next step.** Because this is a new pattern, and you have had three episodes in one month, book an appointment with a doctor or pharmacist. Bring a short diary of dates, how long each headache lasted, and what you took for it. The reference material does not cover what would be causing these headaches in your case, so that assessment needs a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
